// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
// specs/t27os/call_session.t27 -- one T27 OS call as a session a window can drive: dial, ring, answer or decline,
// show the 6-digit code, confirm it, mute, hang up, and a non-blocking poll that says what happened. Host code
// (@extern): build it with t27b, not t27c. Nothing here decides a call state, a code, a key or a replay: those are
// call_core.t27 (ck_next, ck_verdict, the code, the window, the nonces) through the endpoint of
// call_crypto_probe.t27 (ECDH SecKey, HKDF on CCHmac, AES-128-GCM via libffi), and the audio path is the part F/G
// machinery of callback_probe.t27 (capture ring cbp_fin, jitter buffer with the redundant copy and concealment
// cbp_fstore / cbp_fpull / cbp_fout, AudioQueue on the caller's run loop). This file only joins them: one UDP
// socket, the handshake messages and their resends, sealed media frames, events and timeouts.
// Owner, 2026-10-21: calls run inside the OS, explicit consent, never auto-answer. Refs gHashTag/t27#11560.
//
// API for the window. `s` is one block of CS_LEN bytes owned by the window; it must not move while the session is
// open, because the audio callbacks hold the address of its audio part. All calls return at once.
//   cs_open(s, me_fp, bind_ip, port, audio) -> bool   bind one non-blocking UDP socket (ip as a<<24|b<<16|c<<8|d);
//                                                      me_fp is this device's 10-byte fingerprint; audio is
//                                                      CS_AUDIO_DEV (microphone and speakers) or CS_AUDIO_TEST
//                                                      (a numbered test pattern, no devices). State IDLE.
//   cs_call(s, ip, port, peer_fp) -> bool             dial: IDLE / ENDED / FAILED -> CALLING, sends INVITE.
//   cs_poll(s) -> u32                                 read the socket until it is empty, resend unanswered
//                                                      handshake messages every 200 ms, run timeouts, seal and send
//                                                      captured audio, open received audio; returns the oldest
//                                                      unread event (CS_EV_*), CS_EV_NONE when there is none. Call
//                                                      it from the window's main loop at least every 10 ms while a
//                                                      call is ACTIVE (the capture ring holds 70 ms), and on the
//                                                      thread whose run loop runs: the audio callbacks are put on
//                                                      that run loop when the call becomes ACTIVE.
//   cs_state(s) -> u32                                 CK_IDLE .. CK_FAILED of call_core.
//   cs_incoming(s) -> bool                             an incoming call rings (RINGING): show Answer / Decline.
//   cs_peer(s, fp_out) -> u64                          the other party: its 10-byte fingerprint into fp_out and its
//                                                      IPv4 as the result (0 when there is no call).
//   cs_code(s, out) -> bool                            the 6 ASCII digits both screens must show, once known.
//   cs_answer(s) -> bool                               the person pressed Answer: RINGING -> VERIFY, sends ANSWER.
//   cs_decline(s) -> bool                              the person pressed Decline: RINGING -> ENDED.
//   cs_confirm(s, same) -> bool                        the person compared the codes: same = "they match". The call
//                                                      becomes ACTIVE only when both people said match and the
//                                                      peer's sealed CONFIRM names the same transcript; "differ"
//                                                      fails the call at once.
//   cs_mute(s, on) -> bool, cs_muted(s) -> bool        microphone off / on: while muted the frames still go out
//                                                      (the peer's jitter buffer stays fed) but carry silence.
//   cs_hangup(s) -> bool                               cancel a CALLING call, or end VERIFY / ACTIVE: -> ENDED.
//   cs_dismiss(s) -> bool                              the end line was shown: ENDED / FAILED -> IDLE.
//   cs_reason(s) -> u32                                why the last call ended (CS_R_*).
//   cs_stat(s, k) -> u64                               counter k (CS_ST_*), or audio counter i as 1000 + i (the
//                                                      part F header slots of callback_probe.t27).
//   cs_set(s, k, v) -> bool                            a timeout or jitter size (CS_K_*), before or between calls.
//   cs_close(s)                                        stop the audio, close the socket.
// Events: CS_EV_CALLING, CS_EV_RINGING, CS_EV_CODE (the code is ready: show it with Match / Differ), CS_EV_ACTIVE
// (media flows), CS_EV_ENDED, CS_EV_FAILED (read cs_reason), CS_EV_IDLE. A window needs nothing else to draw the
// call: the state, the code, the peer, the reason and the mute flag.
//
// Wire: every message is a call_core frame (52-byte header). INVITE, ANSWER, REVEAL are plain (call_core's
// commitment and transcript bind them); CONFIRM, media and the in-call BYE are sealed (AES-128-GCM, the header is
// the associated data). A media frame's plaintext is part F's packet: audio index u32 LE, length 960 u16, flags
// u16 (1 = the previous frame follows as a redundant copy), 960 bytes PCM, then the previous 960 bytes; sealed it
// is 52 + 1928 + 16 = 1996 bytes. Ending before keys exist (cancel while CALLING, decline while RINGING, hang-up in
// VERIFY) is a plain 52-byte BYE (seq 0) of the call's session, sent three times; it is accepted only before
// ACTIVE, so in a running call only a sealed BYE ends it. A plain BYE can be forged by whoever sees the session id:
// that ends a call that is not yet carrying media, as dropping its packets would; it opens nothing.
// Video (owner, 2026-10-22): the same sealed channel also carries CK_T_VIDEO frames, the parts of a 320x240 JPEG
// (call_core: vc_part / vr_take). cs_set(CS_K_VIDEO, 1) makes an ACTIVE one to one call send 10 frames a second: the
// camera's JPEG while cs_video_feed keeps it fresh, the test generator of call_vid.t27 otherwise. The receiver always
// assembles; cs_vstat(CS_V_*) counts, cs_vlen / s[CS_VLAST ..] give the last complete JPEG, cs_vlive says "video on".
// cs_video_encoder hands in an encoder block (call_vid.t27 vgq_new): the generator's JPEG is then made on its own
// queue and the poll only takes it and cuts it into parts.
// Group video (owner, 2026-10-23): in a group the same switch sends 5 frames a second of 160x120 (quality 0.3, parts
// of 700) to every member whose link is FULL (call_core vg_link: ACTIVE, its sound fresh, few underruns); a PARTIAL
// link gets the sound only. The frame is made once (the encoder queue, or the camera's small JPEG) and sealed once per
// link on that link's key. Every link assembles what its member sends in its own block; csg_vshow names the two links
// whose first frame came first, csg_vlink / csg_vstat count. With CS_K_GSTATS each frame sent is a GVTX line and each
// frame completed a GVRX line on stderr (wall clock ms), so the delay can be read from the two ends.
// Identity (owner, 2026-10-22): cs_identity(s, pub, priv) hands in this device's identity key pair (peer_identity's
// ~/.t27os/identity, the key whose fingerprint was given to cs_open). Once the keys of a call exist, each side sends a
// sealed IDENT (call_core ck_ident_*): its identity key and an ECDSA signature over the session's transcript. The key
// stored for the friend in ~/.t27os/friends (v2) is read when the call starts; peer_identity pid_verdict decides:
// the same key with a signature that verifies -> the session says "match" by itself and no code is shown; no stored
// key, or no proof -> the people compare the code; a stored key and another key or a bad signature -> FAILED with
// CS_R_IDENTITY. After a matched code the proven key of a friend is stored, so the next call needs no code. A message
// session (CS_AUDIO_NONE) has no person to compare a code: it opens only for a trusted friend (else CS_R_UNVERIFIED),
// and cs_confirm(true) is refused there. A block with no identity handed in calls as before, with the code.
// Invitations (owner, 2026-10-22): in a message session the first good proof of a friend whose key is not stored is
// stored (peer_identity pid_store_first: the person pasted that fingerprint), so the session is TRUSTED; the proof of
// someone who is not a friend makes an invitation (pid_is_invitation): the session ends with CS_R_INVITE and
// cs_id_proven gives the window the proven key for invites.t27 (ACCEPT / DECLINE). A call is not changed.
//   cs_id_proven(s, fp, pubk) -> bool                  after CS_R_INVITE: the peer's fingerprint and proven key.
//   cs_identity(s, pubk, priv) -> bool                 the identity key pair for every link of the slice; false when
//                                                      the block is not open or the key is not the one of me_fp.
//   cs_id_state(s) -> u32                              CS_ID_* of the current or last call (unverified, verified,
//                                                      refused, key stored now).
// Beyond the home network (owner, 2026-10-23; call_nat.t27): the call socket also carries the one STUN lookup the
// person asks for and the punch probes. cs_poll takes a datagram that looks like STUN (cn_stun_looks) or is a probe
// (cn_probe_is) off before any call frame is looked at: an answer from the server asked, to the transaction asked,
// gives the public address; every other one is only counted. Neither ever reaches call_core, rings or changes a call.
//   cs_stun_send(s, txid, srv_ip, srv_port) -> bool    one Binding request from the call socket (the window resends).
//   cs_probe_send(s, a) -> bool                        one probe to a (ip * 65536 + port, call_nat cn_punch_ok).
//   cs_nat(s, CS_N_*) -> u64                           the public address and when it came, the counters.
// Behaviour checks, not a security audit.
module T27osCallSession;

